  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nCurrents & Tonight: Another dreary day continues! Taking a look \nacross the area, a brief break continues this afternoon as drier and \ncooler air filters in behind a cold front. This along with breezy \nnorthwesterly surface winds with gust between 25-35 mph has led to \nquite the chilly feel this afternoon with highs sitting in the upper \n40s to mid 50s west of I-29 and the 60s to low 70s east of I-29. \nExpect temperature to continue their gradual decline from here. \nOtherwise, the intermittent showers resume from late afternoon \nonwards as southwest flow aloft helps additional pieces of energy \nlift through areas mainly west of I-29 through Sunday morning with \nmainly light accumulations. Lastly, given the increased cloud cover \novernight thinking temperatures won't decrease as fast nor as far as \nthe NBM thinks (mid 30s to low 40s). With this in mind, decided \nblend in some CONSshort into our MinTs slightly nudge up overnight \ntemperatures into the low to upper 40s \n\nSunday-Monday: Looking into Sunday, another wet and dreary day is \nahead as a subtle 850mb front stalls across our area roughly along a \nWagner to Sioux Falls to Windom, MN line. With broad southwesterly \nflow firmly in place aloft, additional pieces of lift (dPVA) will \nstream into the warm air advective (WAA) regime along and northwest \nof this near-surface feature spurring intermittent showers \nthroughout the day within the left exit region of the coupled jet. \nWhile mostly light accumulation are expected during this period, \nshould see an uptick in coverage from late Sunday evening into \nMonday as additional lift arrives and WAA strengthen ahead of the \ninverted trough axis. Looking aloft, most guidance has the base of \nthe mid-level trough gradually lifting into the Nebraska Pan handle \nby Monday morning then central and eastern SD by Monday afternoon/evening.\nWhile there some questions remains on where the heaviest accumulations\nset up, its worth nothing that NBM QPF has taken a noticable \nnorthwest shift towards the better dyanamics which are west of I-29.\n\nAs such, still thinking most areas receive accumulating rainfall \nthrough Monday. However, currently thinking the axis of 1+ inches \nsets up somewhere west of a Yankton to Sioux Falls to Slayton, MN \nline with the heaviest accumulations west of Highway 281. Everywhere \neast of this line could be affected by a bit more dry air leading to \naccumulations of a 0.50\" of an inch or less. While not a drought \nbuster, this system will provide beneficial rainfall for parts of \nthe area over a 24-36 hour period. With this in mind, the flood \nconcerns are minimal though some ponding will be possible in some \nurban and low lying areas. Lastly, expect the cooler conditions to \npersist for both days given the increasing rain chances with daily \nhighs sitting in the mid 40s to mid 50s north of I-90 and low 50s to \nlow 60s south of I-90. \n\nTuesday Onwards: Heading into the extended period, quieter \nconditions will temporarily return for the first half of Tuesday. \nHowever, additional showers could develop by Tuesday afternoon along \nand east of I-29 as a cold front swings through. From here, the \nquieter conditions should persist through at least Friday as \nsolutions in long-range guidance begins to diverge. Lastly, expect \ndaily highs to remain mainly in the 60s to low 70s with the warmest \nconditions across northwestern IA.",
